# Internationalisation and right-to-left (IN)

This is the short form. Before writing code in this area, read the full rules with reasons, bad/good examples and checks: `.ui-polish/rules/21-internationalisation.md`.

- **IN-01 Logical properties, not left and right** (info). Use logical properties for spacing, borders, radius and position: `margin-inline-start` not `margin-left`, `padding-inline`, `inset-inline-end`, `border-inline-start`, `text-align: start`; in Tailwind `ms-* me-* ps-* pe-* start-* end-* text-start rounded-s-*`. Physical properties are fine for things that do not flip with reading direction (a drop shadow offset, a chart's time axis).
- **IN-02 Text can grow and wrap** (warning). Leave room for translations to be 30–40% longer (up to 200% for short labels): no fixed widths or heights on buttons, tabs, badges and nav items; let them wrap or grow; use `min-width` instead of `width`. Never put words in images, and never build sentences by concatenating translated fragments ("You have " + n + " new messages").
- **IN-03 Direction and language are declared; direction-aware icons flip** (warning). Set `lang` and `dir` on `<html>` from the active locale, and `lang` on any block in another language. Icons that show direction (back and forward arrows, chevrons in breadcrumbs and pagination, "send", progress steps) mirror in right-to-left (`:dir(rtl)` with `scale: -1 1`, or `rtl:-scale-x-100`); icons that do not (play, search, clock, checkmarks, logos, phone numbers, code) do not flip.
- **IN-04 Format with Intl, pluralise with rules** (error). Format numbers, currencies, percentages, dates, times, relative times and lists with `Intl.NumberFormat`, `Intl.DateTimeFormat`, `Intl.RelativeTimeFormat` and `Intl.ListFormat` for the user's locale; sort and compare text with `Intl.Collator`. Choose plural forms with `Intl.PluralRules` or ICU message syntax, never `count === 1 ? '' : 's'`. Store times in UTC and display them in the user's time zone, saying which when it matters.
- **IN-05 Strings live outside the components** (info). In a product that is or will be translated, every user-facing string (labels, placeholders, errors, `aria-label`, `alt`, page titles, emails) comes from the message catalogue, with a key and a note for the translator where the context is not obvious. Keep whole sentences as one message with placeholders. Do not translate data (user names, product SKUs) or code.
- **IN-06 Fonts and line height cover every script** (warning). The font stack covers every script the product ships: add a fallback family for Arabic, Hebrew, Devanagari, CJK and other scripts in use, loaded only for those locales (`unicode-range` subsets). Allow more line height for scripts with tall marks (Arabic, Devanagari, Thai: 1.6–1.8 for body). Do not use letter-spacing or uppercase transforms on scripts that have no case or join their letters.
